WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.439 --> 00:00:12.940
In 1962 the United States Air Force was working
furiously to develop an ejection system for

00:00:12.940 --> 00:00:16.090
pilots of the new Convair B-58 Hustler.

00:00:16.090 --> 00:00:19.210
The first jet bomber capable of mach 2 flight.

00:00:19.210 --> 00:00:24.029
Bailing out at the speeds and altitudes the
B-58 was designed for with conventional ejection

00:00:24.029 --> 00:00:28.150
seats of that era would have resulted, at
best, n severe injury.

00:00:28.150 --> 00:00:32.070
Windblast would have likely rip your helmet
and air supply off, the force of air would

00:00:32.070 --> 00:00:36.960
violently contort your limbs, with the explosive
thrust of the ejection seat compressing your

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:39.110
spine, and if all that didn’t kill you.

00:00:39.110 --> 00:00:43.200
You may just freeze to death, with eternal
temperatures dipping to as low as -50 degrees

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:44.239
celsius.

00:00:44.239 --> 00:00:49.309
And so the Stanley Aviation Company and Convair
began a partnership to develop a fully enclosed

00:00:49.309 --> 00:00:53.569
escape capsule, which would fold shut before
ejection and shelter the crewmen from the

00:00:53.569 --> 00:00:57.569
environment, but they couldn’t start testing
this new concept on humans.

00:00:57.569 --> 00:01:00.662
The conditions were simply too hostile to
risk a human on, so they came to the next

00:01:00.662 --> 00:01:01.662
logical alternative.

00:01:01.662 --> 00:01:05.040
The United States Air Force strapped a drug
up Black Bear called Yogi into the capsule

00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:08.810
and launched it at 35,000 feet at supersonic
speeds.

00:01:08.810 --> 00:01:09.810
[1]

00:01:09.810 --> 00:01:13.550
So how the hell did the world of aviation
come to the point of needing to fire live

00:01:13.550 --> 00:01:15.980
black bears at supersonic speeds?

00:01:15.980 --> 00:01:18.780
Ejection seats were not always necessary in
flight.

00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:24.300
At the relatively slow speeds of WW1 and early
WW2 era planes pilots could simply unstrap

00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:29.050
their restraints and jump overboard, like
this German pilot being pursued by an RAF

00:01:29.050 --> 00:01:30.050
Tempest.

00:01:30.050 --> 00:01:33.920
But as the war progressed and jet propulsion
technology advanced, British Intelligence

00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:38.340
began to receiving reports of German pilots
being “fired into the sky” from defeated

00:01:38.340 --> 00:01:39.610
German jets.

00:01:39.610 --> 00:01:44.630
As usual, the Germans were well head of the
competition in experimenting with new technology,

00:01:44.630 --> 00:01:48.750
and the medical branch of the Luftwaffe was
busy testing the physical limits of the human

00:01:48.750 --> 00:01:50.990
body to withstand G forces.

00:01:50.990 --> 00:01:54.320
The designers of these devices had a difficult
balancing act to co-ordinate.

00:01:54.320 --> 00:01:59.420
It was essential that the ejection provided
enough acceleration for the pilot to safely

00:01:59.420 --> 00:02:03.980
clear the aircraft, while not accelerating
them so fast that they could suffer injury.

00:02:03.980 --> 00:02:08.720
The first thing these designers needed to
consider was how to power a device like this.

00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:12.920
They needed a way of storing potential energy
that could be released rapidly in order to

00:02:12.920 --> 00:02:17.020
achieve the necessary acceleration in an emergency
situation.

00:02:17.020 --> 00:02:21.520
Early designs simple called the pilot to manually
release the canopy before triggering a compressed

00:02:21.520 --> 00:02:25.600
spring that would hopefully launch the pilot
clear of the plane like some sort of Wile

00:02:25.600 --> 00:02:27.020
Coyote scene.

00:02:27.020 --> 00:02:29.260
This had some obvious inherent flaws.

00:02:29.260 --> 00:02:33.420
The springs did not possess the necessary
potential energy to launch the weight of the

00:02:33.420 --> 00:02:38.320
pilot and seat upwards to safety, and requiring
the pilot to manually release the canopy in

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:41.420
an emergency situation was incredibly dangerous.

00:02:41.420 --> 00:02:46.370
As Douglas Davie discovered to his demise
in 1944, during a test flight of the the British

00:02:46.370 --> 00:02:49.450
prototype twin-engined jet fighter, the Meteor.

00:02:49.450 --> 00:02:53.660
While performing a high speed test the aircraft
lost one of it’s engines sending it into

00:02:53.660 --> 00:02:57.290
an uncontrollable spin due to differential
thrust.

00:02:57.290 --> 00:03:02.430
At this time Britain had not put serious consideration
into escape mechanisms, and Davie was forced

00:03:02.430 --> 00:03:06.130
to open the canopy manually, which snapped
shut and severed his arm.

00:03:06.130 --> 00:03:10.560
He was then either then thrown out of the
plane by the g-forces of the hurtling aircraft,

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:12.819
or somehow managed to haul himself out.

00:03:12.819 --> 00:03:15.690
Only to be critically injured by the aircraft’s
tailplane. [2]

00:03:15.690 --> 00:03:20.209
This gruesome event highlighted to the British
the need to develop ejection mechanisms, and

00:03:20.209 --> 00:03:23.720
with the end of the war coming, they would
have plenty of German engineering to gain

00:03:23.720 --> 00:03:25.120
inspiration from.

00:03:25.120 --> 00:03:29.140
The Germans were racing ahead of the competition,
thanks to the huge amount of resources the

00:03:29.140 --> 00:03:31.900
Germans poured into experimental aircraft.

00:03:31.900 --> 00:03:36.890
By definition, these aircraft were unpredictable,
and so the Germans also lead the way in developing

00:03:36.890 --> 00:03:38.640
escape mechanisms.

00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:42.640
Helping save talented test pilots like Helmut
Schenk, who was the first man in history to

00:03:42.640 --> 00:03:49.340
use an ejection seat in an emergency situation,
while testing the Heinkel He-280 jet fighter.

00:03:49.340 --> 00:03:53.160
This seat did not use springs to power the
ejection, but compressed air.

00:03:53.160 --> 00:03:57.569
An important improvement in energy storage,
but required a significant amount of additional

00:03:57.569 --> 00:04:02.000
mechanisms and heavy parts to operate, like
valves and pressurised air tanks.

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:06.390
Despite the relatively cumbersome design of
these ejections seats, by the end of the war

00:04:06.390 --> 00:04:11.900
it is reported that up to 60 luftwaffe airmen
had used these devices to escape their aircraft,

00:04:11.900 --> 00:04:16.890
and every experimental aircraft the Germans
tested came equipped with an ejection seat.

00:04:16.890 --> 00:04:20.880
After the war the Allies took over as the
forerunners of ejection seat design.

00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:23.790
Most notably by an Irish engineer, James Martin.

00:04:23.790 --> 00:04:27.580
Who founded the Martin-Baker company alongside
Captain Valentine Baker.

00:04:27.580 --> 00:04:32.250
They conducted their own tests of human endurance
using this tripod launcher.

00:04:32.250 --> 00:04:35.660
Another Irish man, Bernard Lynch, being the
brave human guinea pig.

00:04:35.660 --> 00:04:40.241
They used an explosive cartridge to power
the launch, instead of compressed air, and

00:04:40.241 --> 00:04:42.520
gradually upped the explosive power.

00:04:42.520 --> 00:04:47.120
With explosive upward trajectories like, injury
becomes much more likely if the pilot does

00:04:47.120 --> 00:04:52.470
not remain upright in his seat, a difficult
task when under such immense g-forces.

00:04:52.470 --> 00:04:57.430
And so Martin Baker cleverly built the trigger
into a retractable head cover which restrained

00:04:57.430 --> 00:05:01.880
the pilots head in an upright position, and
protected it from windblast.

00:05:01.880 --> 00:05:06.230
While revolutionary in it’s design this
ejection seat was incredibly simple compared

00:05:06.230 --> 00:05:08.260
to modern ejection seats.

00:05:08.260 --> 00:05:12.530
Every step of the process was manually except
for the deployment of the drogue shoot, which

00:05:12.530 --> 00:05:17.460
was triggered when a fifteen foot wire attached
to the firing mechanism and the cockpit floor

00:05:17.460 --> 00:05:19.880
reached the end of it’s line on ejection.

00:05:19.880 --> 00:05:24.810
The pilot needed to jettison the canopy manually,
pull the face curtain trigger, wait as the

00:05:24.810 --> 00:05:29.580
drogue shoot decelerated and stabilised his
fall, then unbuckle his belts and jump clear

00:05:29.580 --> 00:05:32.130
of the seat to deploy his own parachute.

00:05:32.130 --> 00:05:36.780
An impossible task if the pilot was knocked
unconscious during the ejection sequence.

00:05:36.780 --> 00:05:40.970
Martin-Baker won a contract with the RAF for
all future aircraft to be fitted with this

00:05:40.970 --> 00:05:46.740
first generation seat, and it saved it’s
first life in 1949 when Jo Lancaster was testing

00:05:46.740 --> 00:05:53.180
the Armstrong Whitsworth A.W.52, an experimental
flying wing aircraft, which entered an uncontrollable

00:05:53.180 --> 00:05:57.460
pitch oscillation due to aerodynamic flutter
of it’s elevons.

00:05:57.460 --> 00:06:01.360
Lancaster escaped and survived to tell the
story, but there was plenty of work left to

00:06:01.360 --> 00:06:02.360
do.

00:06:02.360 --> 00:06:06.509
Lancaster was lucky, as the problem was encountered
at relatively high altitude.

00:06:06.509 --> 00:06:11.900
Leaving him plenty of time to deploy his parachute,
but most problems occur during take off and

00:06:11.900 --> 00:06:17.270
landing, and so the next vital step in ejection
seat design was developing a zero-zero ejection

00:06:17.270 --> 00:06:18.270
seat.

00:06:18.270 --> 00:06:23.169
A seat capable of saving the pilots life at
zero velocity, and zero altitude.

00:06:23.169 --> 00:06:26.449
Here we are beginning to strike the limit
of human endurance.

00:06:26.449 --> 00:06:31.560
To safely evacuate an aircraft at an altitude
this low would require significant sustained

00:06:31.560 --> 00:06:35.840
acceleration to ensure clearance of a crash
and an explosion, and provide enough height

00:06:35.840 --> 00:06:37.870
to safely deploy a parachute.

00:06:37.870 --> 00:06:41.850
With tolerances this tight we first need to
automate more steps.

00:06:41.850 --> 00:06:47.020
By the 1960s both Martin-Baker, and their
American counterpart Weber were developing

00:06:47.020 --> 00:06:48.400
these zero-zero seats.

00:06:48.400 --> 00:06:53.060
[5] By this time Webers seats had saved over
500 lives, and had been fitted in aircraft

00:06:53.060 --> 00:06:56.380
like the f-106 and the gemini space capsule.

00:06:56.380 --> 00:07:02.410
The F-106 served in the US Air Force for over
40 years and during that time was fitted with

00:07:02.410 --> 00:07:04.420
three different ejection seats.

00:07:04.420 --> 00:07:10.120
The interim seat, the Supersonic Rotational
B-seat, and finally the zero-zero Rocat. [7]

00:07:10.120 --> 00:07:14.070
Each showing the gradual change in design
ethos to what would eventually become the

00:07:14.070 --> 00:07:16.890
modern day standard design template.

00:07:16.890 --> 00:07:20.810
The interim seat was highly automated, with
a carefully choreographed ballet of mechanical

00:07:20.810 --> 00:07:21.810
triggers

00:07:21.810 --> 00:07:26.250
like an advanced rube goldberg machine, all
starting with the pilot pulling these ejection

00:07:26.250 --> 00:07:27.330
handles.

00:07:27.330 --> 00:07:31.479
Triggering an explosive initiator [5] to fire
which would began a chain reaction of events

00:07:31.479 --> 00:07:35.110
through the hot gas emanating its fix volume
chamber.

00:07:35.110 --> 00:07:39.639
This hot gas would flow through ballistic
hoses to other firing pins in propellant powered

00:07:39.639 --> 00:07:43.260
actuators, the pressure of the gas would remove
the firing pins.

00:07:43.260 --> 00:07:47.530
Allowing the actuators to fire and unlatch
the canopy, and forcefully remove it from

00:07:47.530 --> 00:07:48.740
the aircraft.

00:07:48.740 --> 00:07:52.610
As the canopy cleared the aircraft it would
pull a lanyard, which would trigger the explosive

00:07:52.610 --> 00:07:55.150
cartridge that would lift the seat clear of
the plane.

00:07:55.150 --> 00:07:59.200
As the seat travelled up the rails it would
impact another trigger firing a final time

00:07:59.200 --> 00:08:03.790
delayed initiator, which would activate the
drogue shoot and release the seat buckles.

00:08:03.790 --> 00:08:08.160
The pilot would then either freefall, or,
if they had attached their zero delay lanyard,

00:08:08.160 --> 00:08:12.169
would have their parachute deployed automatically
as they fell free of the seat.

00:08:12.169 --> 00:08:16.940
This carefully timed sequence was a massive
improvement to manual steps of old, but it

00:08:16.940 --> 00:08:18.650
was missing two vital things.

00:08:18.650 --> 00:08:23.610
It was not capable of supersonic ejections
or zero-zero ejections.

00:08:23.610 --> 00:08:28.350
Initially pilots pushed for supersonic capabilities,
and so one of the most intricate and fascinating

00:08:28.350 --> 00:08:31.680
ejection seats in history, the B-seat, was
developed.

00:08:31.680 --> 00:08:35.930
This seat was designed to raise and rotate
into the airstream, where the bottom of the

00:08:35.930 --> 00:08:40.340
seat would protect the crew from airblast
and act as an aerodynamic surface to provide

00:08:40.340 --> 00:08:43.289
lift, raising the seat away from the aircraft.

00:08:43.289 --> 00:08:47.570
At this speed the seats need extra lift to
ensure they will clear the plane on time,

00:08:47.570 --> 00:08:51.660
and to help with that goal rocket motors would
fire to lift the seat to safety.

00:08:51.660 --> 00:08:56.910
The seat had two telescoping booms that helped
stop the seat from rolling violently on ejection.

00:08:56.910 --> 00:09:01.890
This was an ingenious design, but absurdly
complicated with over 30 pyrotechnic devices

00:09:01.890 --> 00:09:05.700
from initiators, explosive bolts and wire
cutters.

00:09:05.700 --> 00:09:10.149
This complexity led to a number of fatal ejections,
and with mounting statistical evidence that

00:09:10.149 --> 00:09:15.110
supersonic ejections were much less likely
than a slow and low ejection, a replacement

00:09:15.110 --> 00:09:16.470
was requested.

00:09:16.470 --> 00:09:21.550
And thus the zero-zero seat was developed,
using the interim seat as a base.

00:09:21.550 --> 00:09:26.220
Adding rocket motors to provide the necessary
height to clear the plane after the initial

00:09:26.220 --> 00:09:30.770
ejection, and adding an explosively deployed
parachute to ensure it opened quickly.

00:09:30.770 --> 00:09:35.560
Unlike the escape capsule of the B-58 this
system was not tested with a bear.

00:09:35.560 --> 00:09:38.430
It was tested by a lunatic by the name of
Jim Hall.

00:09:38.430 --> 00:09:42.200
Jim Hall is quoted saying “I have been kicked
in the ass harder than that”.

00:09:42.200 --> 00:09:46.310
This may well have been the source of the
liar liar pants on fire phrase.

00:09:46.310 --> 00:09:51.240
This zero-zero design has been the template
for all ejection seats since, with Martin-Baker

00:09:51.240 --> 00:09:56.390
becoming the world leading manufacturer adding
incremental improvements over the past 60

00:09:56.390 --> 00:09:57.390
years.

00:09:57.390 --> 00:10:00.970
Adding sensors and flight computers, along
with vectoring nozzles for the rockets to

00:10:00.970 --> 00:10:05.940
ensure the optimum trajectory of the seat,
even with the plane rolled upside down.

00:10:05.940 --> 00:10:10.190
Incremental improvement like this over the
years leads to a vastly better product, and

00:10:10.190 --> 00:10:13.740
